## Not So Average After All

“Hey, Matt. You’re a data guy, right?” I shrugged and nodded at the same time, acknowledging that I *am* a data guy, but I was reluctant to allow myself to be reduced to such a singular description. “What’s the average net worth of an American family?” 

Having recently reviewed the Federal Reserve’s 2022 release of its *Survey of Consumer Finances*, I responded, “The *average* net worth was a little more than $1 million in 2022, but…” 

Before I could qualify my answer, Martin, a colleague with whom several others and I were having lunch, contorted his face into a look representing some mixture of confusion, disbelief, and apprehension as he exclaimed, “What!?! There’s no way that’s true!” 

I calmly finished my statement by saying, “…the *median* net worth was only about $192,000.” Martin looked relieved. He was about five years away from retirement, and didn’t mind telling us that a rough estimate of his own net worth comfortably exceeded that figure. 

“So, I’m above average! Not bad!” he declared. 

"Well, actually..." Not wanting to come across as pedantic or fastidious, I left it there.

I’ve had many conversations like this over the course of my career as a data scientist, and I’ve found that people have a strong desire or need to summarize and compress information into a single value, particularly for purposes of comparison. It’s just easier to reason about a problem when we can compare \( x \) to \( y \) and see which one is larger or smaller. Our brains summarize automatically, and we can only hold a small handful of items in our short-term memory at any given moment in time. Psychologists once put the number at seven, give or take a couple, but the modern estimate is closer to just four.<Note>George A. Miller, “The Magical Number Seven, Plus or Minus Two: Some Limits on Our Capacity for Processing Information,” <em>Psychological Review</em> 63, no. 2 (1956): 81–97; Nelson Cowan, “The Magical Number 4 in Short-Term Memory: A Reconsideration of Mental Storage Capacity,” <em>Behavioral and Brain Sciences</em> 24, no. 1 (2001): 87–114.</Note> Either way, it’s not a lot. Given this constraint, it makes sense for us to condense information to make it easier to work through a problem. 

The trouble with this, and with Martin’s question, is that wealth is not normally distributed. The net worth distribution doesn't conform to a nicely shaped, symmetrical bell curve with a single peak and few values that lie far from the center. In truth, around 7.5% of families have negative net worth, most have a relatively small net worth, and a tiny fraction of American families are worth billions of dollars. If we plot an estimate of the wealth distribution for American families using the Fed’s 2022 data, as well as the average (mean) and median values, it looks like this:

<Density
	id="net-worth"
	data={netWorth}
	x="x"
	y="density"
	below="share_below"
	xLabel="Net worth"
	xFormat="$.3~s"
	markers={[{ x: s.median, label: 'Median' }, { x: s.mean, label: 'Mean' }]}
	title="Net Worth of U.S. Families, 2022"
	source="Federal Reserve, Survey of Consumer Finances"
	period="2022"
	note="Families worth more than $2.5 million extend off the right edge"
/>

The line in <Fig ref="net-worth" /> shows how common net worths near each value on the horizontal axis are. Another way to think about it is that the area under the curve between two points on the horizontal axis represents the share of families whose net worth lies within that range. We see a sharp peak, fairly close to the $0 mark, and that the distribution extends outward to the right with a long tail. Notice that the horizontal axis doesn't go beyond $2.5 million; in reality, the tail is much longer. The wealthiest family in the survey's public data was worth approximately $2.4 billion. To show that value on a chart with a linear scale on a computer screen, one would need a *very* wide monitor. In fact, if the distance from $0 to the $2.5 million mark measures four inches on your screen, the $2.4 billion mark would lie 320 feet away --- more than the length of a football field! Observe also that the difference between the median and the mean is around $867,000. This shows the importance of not conflating the concepts of mean and median, as Martin did in the conversation above.

So what do we want to know, then, when we ask about the average? We are probably asking about what's *typical* or *most common*, and that is sometimes very different from the statistical mean. Looking at the distribution of net worth in <Fig ref="net-worth" />, many of us might believe some value close to the peak is most representative of an American family's net worth. In that case, we might consider the *mode* of the distribution, or the most frequently occurring value. In this case, the mode is just a few thousand dollars. This presents its own problem, though. About six out of every seven families have a net worth that exceeds the mode. Does it make sense to call it the most representative value when it sits below almost everyone? 

This is precisely the challenge that arises when trying to characterize non-normally distributed data with a single summary statistic. Take a moment to think about the median and mean values in <Fig ref="net-worth" />. Does either seem better suited than the other to characterize the *typical* American family's net worth?

Maybe the problem is that for certain distributions, the very idea of a characteristic, typical, usual, or common value simply doesn't apply. What do we do in these cases? These questions and ideas, among others, are what we will explore in this series. Before we begin digging into these issues, however, let's take a look at how normally distributed data arise, and why simple averages do a good job of summarizing them.

## True or False

There are many processes that can be measured with a simple binary outcome: either true or false, yes or no, success or failure. Businesses market to customers via email campaigns and a customer either opens the email, or doesn't. Website visitors either make a purchase, or they leave without buying anything. In quality control processes, a product passes a quality test, or it fails. Applications for credit are approved or they are denied. Soccer players (or footballers, depending on where you are in the world) taking penalty kicks either score, or they don't.

Statisticians call these kinds of processes *Bernoulli trials*, named after the (in)famous 17th-century Swiss mathematician, Jacob Bernoulli. If we record each success as a \( 1 \), and each failure as a \( 0 \), the average is nothing more than the proportion of successes, calculated by taking the sum and dividing it by the total number of trials. As the number of trials increases, that proportion settles toward the true probability of success, often represented as \( p \).

To see this in action, let's consider a soccer player taking penalty kicks. As we've already established, each attempt is a Bernoulli trial and it results in either a goal being scored, or no goal. Let's assume that the true, underlying probability of this player scoring on any given attempt is 75%. In the top half of <Fig ref="kicks" />, we see the running calculation of the player's average, which we can call \( \hat{p} \) (pronounced *p-hat*), and how it evolves as the number of penalty kicks taken increases. After the first three kicks, \( \hat{p} \) is 100%, and then it slumps down to 62% after 26 kicks before trending upwards toward \( p \) as the number of attempts increases. In the bottom half, we see how \( \hat{p} \) evolves over a much larger number of trials, and we can see that the average success rate stabilizes after a couple of hundred measurements and remains tantalizingly close to \( p \), the true probability of success. 

Notice the gray-shaded region on the left-hand side of this plot. The average success rate is very unstable here and tends to swing dramatically as more attempts are added into the mix. This is a sample size effect. If we estimate the probability of success using a small sample, we end up with very different results. As sample sizes get bigger and bigger, our results begin to settle down and calculations from one large sample don't differ much from the others. This is the *law of large numbers* at work, and Bernoulli himself was the first to prove it. It's responsible for \( \hat{p} \) gravitating toward \( p \).

<RunningAverage
	id="kicks"
	data={kicks}
	p={k.p}
	strip={40}
	unit="kicks"
	successLabel="Scored"
	failureLabel="Missed"
	notes={[{ x: 26, text: 'a slump: 62% after 26 kicks', dx: 18, dy: 22 }]}
	title="The Share of Goals Settles Toward p"
	source="Simulated"
	note="1,000 penalty kicks by a player who scores 75% of the time"
/>

## Buckets of Success

Suppose we take some number of penalty kick outcomes and put them into a bucket, the size of which we'll just represent as \( n \). As we just established, the success rates for buckets with small \( n \) will tend to vary quite a bit more from bucket to bucket than those with large \( n \). With \( n = 1 \), for example, there are only two possible values a bucket can have: 100% or 0%. If \( n = 2 \), a bucket's success rate can be 0%, 50%, or 100%. As \( n \) increases, the number of possible success rates increases. 

Consider the following experiment. We use buckets that contain either 1, 12, 40, or 100 kicks. For each bucket size, we use 10,000 separate buckets. In other words, we have 10,000 buckets that each contain a single penalty kick outcome, another 10,000 buckets that each contain the outcomes from a dozen kicks, and so on.

For our \( n = 1 \) buckets (remember, each of these just tells us whether a single penalty kick resulted in a goal), we observe that 24.7% of the kicks were unsuccessful and 75.3% produced a goal. We see this in the top-left panel of <Fig ref="buckets" />. There are two bars in this simple histogram, one for each outcome (either 0% or 100%) and the height of each bar corresponds to the percentage of the 10,000 buckets that yielded the corresponding outcome. 

In the top-right panel, we have a similar plot, just with more bars since there are more possible outcomes with the larger bucket size of 12 kicks. In this plot, only 4% of buckets had a success rate of 50%, while the most common outcome (26.4% of buckets) was a success rate of 75%. 

<HistogramPanels
	id="buckets"
	data={buckets}
	panel="size"
	x="share"
	y="freq"
	curve={bucketCurve}
	panelTitle={(n) => `Buckets of ${n} ${n === 1 ? 'kick' : 'kicks'}`}
	xDomain={[0, 1]}
	xTicks={[0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1]}
	xFormat=".0%"
	yFormat=".1%"
	reference={k.p}
	referenceLabel="p"
	yName="Share of buckets"
	xLabel="Share of goals in the bucket"
	title="As Buckets Grow, the Shares Bunch Up Into a Bell"
	source="Simulated"
	note="10,000 buckets of each size; each panel has its own vertical scale"
/>

Moving down to the bottom row, we see a very intriguing shape has emerged with our \( n = 40 \) buckets. The largest share of the 10,000 buckets (15%) had a success rate of 75%, and all of our buckets tend to cluster around that figure, approaching a shape that looks like a normal distribution! 

The final histogram calculated from the 10,000 buckets of 100 kicks each shows the same phenomenon as its bars conform to the smooth curve of the normal distribution even more closely.

So what does this mean? Can we summarize our soccer player's penalty kick success rate (across many sets of 100 kicks) using a single number? We can, and <Fig ref="bell" /> shows why. It depicts a normal distribution of penalty kick success rates with a mean success rate of 75% and a standard deviation of 4.3 percentage points. We haven't discussed standard deviations yet, so for now, just think of the standard deviation as how spread out the distribution is. Large standard deviations yield shorter, wider distributions while smaller standard deviations produce taller, narrower distributions. 

<Density
	id="bell"
	data={bell}
	x="x"
	y="density"
	xLabel="Share of goals in the bucket"
	xFormat=".0%"
	belowLabel="Share of buckets below"
	markers={[{ x: 'mean' }, { x: 'median' }, { x: 'mode' }]}
	title="In a Normal Distribution, the Three Averages Agree"
	note="Normal curve for buckets of 100 kicks: mean 75%, standard deviation 4.3 points"
/>

This distribution shows us why it's sometimes okay to use the word *average* so loosely. Average is a slippery word, and people using it might be referring to the mean, median, or mode. When data are distributed normally, that's completely acceptable --- they are in fact all the same value, as we see in <Fig ref="bell" />.

## The Central Idea

Let's step back for a moment and briefly chronicle our journey from simple binary outcomes to a bell curve whose measures of central tendency (mean, median, and mode) are the same. In the top-left panel of <Fig ref="buckets" />, we saw a histogram of single kick outcomes, and it looked nothing like a bell curve. However, once we started averaging over an increasing number of penalty kicks, those averages started bunching up into a bell shape. 

This isn't a peculiarity of penalty kicks --- it's a mathematical result that statisticians call the *Central Limit Theorem*. If you add up or average enough independent random pieces, the results tend toward a bell curve as you include more of them, almost regardless of what those individual pieces look like as long as certain conditions are met (note that computing a sum and an average are closely related; one is just divided by the count while the other is not). For example, consider the distribution of possible outcomes when rolling a die. For a single die, the distribution is flat, or uniform, across the discrete values of one to six. However, if you roll ten dice and take the sum, the distribution forms a bell curve (centered on 35 in this case). Another example might be a store's daily sales total. The totals tend to form a bell curve, even though individual purchases are very uneven. A typical store might make many small sales in a day and just a few big sales, but because each day's total is the sum of many purchases, the totals look like a normal distribution from one ordinary day to the next. Human heights (for a given sex) are also approximately normally distributed, as they result from adding the effects of many genetic and environmental factors, each of which either increases or decreases an individual's height relative to a baseline.

## The Fine Print

At this point you may be slightly confused. This essay opened with an example of data that are *not* distributed normally, but we just saw that the bell curve arises when we take sums or averages of independent random variables, regardless of what they look like individually. So why didn't it work with the net worth distribution?

Before we answer that, let me provide you with a checklist of sorts that we can use to determine whether we should expect a process to result in a normal distribution. A *value* in the checklist is an aggregation of *pieces.* For example, one bucket's success rate is a *value* built from individual penalty kicks, or *pieces*. 

<FieldGuide id="bell-checklist" title="Should I Expect a Bell Curve?">
<Section title="How each value is built">
<Check label="Many pieces">Does each value consist of many distinct pieces rather than a few?</Check>
<Check label="Additive">Does each piece add to the total rather than multiplying it?</Check>
<Check label="Independent">Is each piece roughly independent of the others, rather than driven by them?</Check>
<Check label="No giants">Is every piece small relative to the total, so that no single piece can overwhelm the rest?</Check>
</Section>
<Section title="Whether the values belong together">
<Check label="One group">Do all the values come from one group as opposed to a mix of groups with different central tendencies?</Check>
</Section>
<p><em>All yes:</em> expect something close to a bell, with a single peak, a roughly symmetric shape, and few values far from the mean. <em>Any no:</em> don't count on a bell; it may warp, fracture, or never form at all.</p>
</FieldGuide>

In the case of our penalty kick experiment with the \( n = 100 \) buckets, we met the many pieces criterion (100 kicks per bucket), each piece was strictly additive (a single kick could only add zero or one to the total), our kicks were independent (we assume the result of one kick didn't influence another), each piece was small (no penalty kick ever results in more than one goal), and they all came from a single player. This results in *yes* answers to all of the questions in our checklist, and it's why we saw the normal distribution emerge from the data.

Returning to our question about the net worth distribution, wealth fails several items on our checklist. Most importantly, it isn't built by adding up many small pieces like our penalty kicks. Wealth grows through a multiplicative process, since returns compound and tomorrow's gain depends on today's value. This also touches on the independence test. Advantages tend to build on one another, so families that start with a large inheritance, for example, are more likely to build even more wealth over time. The Fed's survey also mixes families at very different stages of life, from young couples who have only just begun to save to retirees near their lifetime peak, so the data fail our one-group test. In Part 2, we'll look at a less extreme version of this with wage distributions, where we'll explore these failures in more depth.

Our checklist in <Fig ref="bell-checklist" /> is intended to be concise, and it is not an exhaustive test that will work in every situation. However, I think it covers most of the cases that most readers will encounter in their daily lives, and it is therefore an incredibly useful tool. As you progress through the rest of the series, we'll come back to it, and you should challenge yourself to work through it for the various data generating processes we will explore. This will help build a strong intuition for whether you can expect data to be normally distributed, which will in turn help you decide whether simple averages are sufficient for summarizing the data, or whether you will need to reach for other tools that we will cover later in the series. 

## Crooked Averages

So what happens when we compute an average for non-normal data? Assuming the arithmetic is performed accurately, you will end up with a number that is correct, at least mathematically. The issue is that the average is a summary, with strings attached, and those strings represent conditions that are often not met in the real world. When this occurs, the average, correct as it may be, simply isn't telling you the whole story. Or even worse, it may even be telling you the *wrong* story.

I stopped short of explaining these ideas to Martin because he seemed quite content to compare his own net worth to the median value, and it wasn't the time or place to get into the weeds about the shape of the net worth distribution. However, you may now be able to imagine exactly what I would have told him. The net worth distribution is not normal, so one cannot safely conflate the concepts of mean, median, or mode. Martin declared that he was above average after learning that the *median* value was roughly $192,000. His net worth, accumulated over decades of (non-additive) compounding, is in fact *below* average, but he's in the top half of the net worth distribution, which is indeed a nice place to be.

In Parts 2 and 3 of this series, we will explore distributions that, like the net worth distribution, are not normal. We will discover how to recognize data-generating processes that follow these non-normal distributions, and how to better characterize these data to make better decisions. In Part 2, in addition to wage distributions, we'll look at home values, which tend to be heavily skewed much like our net worth distribution from this essay. We'll see how the average compares to other statistics and how we can better tell the story of these distributions by simply looking at them from different perspectives.